%erg 0.1
Title: the asset invariant suite cannot see a revert narrated as a refresh
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-16T12:42Z claude created

--- body ---

## Context

`tests/test_assetinvariant.sh` landed with PR #352 as ticket 0278's integration
guard: three fixture stores (clean, edited, stampless) driven through `erg
init`, `erg update` and `erg migrate`, auditing both halves of the whole-class
invariant after every command.

Review round 2 of that PR arrived after it merged. Its red team built a mutant
binary forcing `downgrade := false` at `src/go/init.go:267` -- which makes erg
narrate a revert as an ordinary refresh, the exact thing ticket 0279 was filed
and fixed for -- and **the suite passed it 42/0**.

The cause is a missing fixture, not a weak assertion. Reaching the direction
logic needs a store whose stamp RECORDS the bytes on disk and postdates the
running binary: `isCleanUpgrade` consults `isRollback` only after
`diskHash == stampedHash`. No fixture in the suite stamped newer than the
binary, so `stampedByNewer` was false everywhere, `downgrade` was false
everywhere, and the word "downgraded" was decoration.

`tests/test_init.sh` still catches the mutant at the single-command tier. What
is missing is the cross-command tier -- which is the one place 0278's invariant
is stated, and rollback is the only path in its set demonstrated to lose data.

## Relevant files

- `tests/test_assetinvariant.sh` -- the suite; a fourth arm goes here
- `src/go/manifest.go` -- `isRollback`, `looksLikeBuildDate`, and why the stamp
  date must match `buildDateLayout` byte for byte or the arm silently degrades
  to an ordinary local-edit arm
- `src/go/init.go` -- `stampedByNewer`, `downgrade`, and the `rollbackPreserved`
  exemption

## Actions

1. Add a `rollback` fixture state: both managed assets diverged, `.erg-assets`
   recording the on-disk hashes, `date:` set to a future stamp of exactly
   `buildDateLayout`'s shape.
2. Guard the fixture in `assert_state` -- a stamp that does not match the disk
   sends the arm down the local-edit path, where it would pass while proving
   nothing about direction.
3. Drive it through check, init, update and migrate like the other three.
4. Bump the suite's coverage pin.

## Test

Red step, and it is the mutant itself: rebuild with `downgrade := false` at
`init.go:267` and confirm the new arm fails on
"rollback/migrate: the revert is narrated as a downgrade, not as a refresh",
then revert and confirm green. A rollback arm that passes against that mutant
has not been written yet.

## Verification

- [ ] `downgrade := false` mutant fails the suite
- [ ] unmutated suite green, coverage pin matches the new count
- [ ] `make check` exit 0

## Invariants

- The three existing arms keep their assertions and their counts.
- migrate's force-overwrite of a diverged AGENTS.md stays asserted as the
  WANTED, announced outcome (charter 0224) in the rollback arm too -- there it
  must be announced as a *downgrade*, which is the whole point.
- Nothing in the new arm pins behaviour ticket 0296 intends to change.

## Exit criteria

- A revert narrated as a refresh fails this suite

## Related

- 0278 -- the tracker whose integration pass this suite is
- 0279 -- the defect class the arm exercises
- 0296 -- the real defect the arm's fixture uncovered, left unfixed here
